    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you help me draft a quick summary for my CTO? I need to explain the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling in AWS, specifically regarding our microservices architecture. Keep it professional and high-level so a non-technical stakeholder can understand it, but make sure the distinction between adding more instances vs. increasing instance size is clear.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For your CTO, you can frame it this way: Vertical scaling is like giving a single, very powerful server a bigger brain and more muscle—you're making one existing machine stronger. Horizontal scaling, on the other hand, is like adding more workers to a team; you're spreading the load across multiple, smaller, but numerous instances. In our microservices setup, we often lean towards horizontal scaling because it's more resilient, preventing a single point of failure from bringing down the whole system. This approach is generally preferred for handling the unpredictable traffic spikes we see.",
      "source": "target",
